# Local settings of the Worker for `pnpm dev` and `pnpm preview`. Optional:
# without a .dev.vars file, sign-in is simulated and the admin is
# admin@example.com. To change that, copy this file to .dev.vars, which git
# ignores. `pnpm e2e` and `pnpm preview:e2e` never read it.

# Who can always sign in, as an admin. Comma-separated, each entry a bare
# email address. Any other entry is ignored, with an error in the log.
ADMIN_EMAILS=admin@example.com

# The OAuth client of a Google Cloud project. With both set, sign-in goes to
# the real Google instead of the simulated one. The client needs the redirect
# address http://localhost:5173/api/auth/google/callback, and for
# `pnpm preview` the same on port 4173.
#GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
#GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The keys that the credentials of connected accounts are sealed with before
# they are stored. Without this line, `pnpm dev` uses a key from the
# repository, which is fine for trying things out and protects nothing.
# `pnpm preview` does not: it serves a production build, which has no such
# key, and without this line it refuses to connect accounts, with a 503. A
# comma-separated list of "<version>:<key>", each key 32 random bytes in
# base64: make one with `openssl rand -base64 32`. The highest version
# seals. To replace a key, add one with a higher version and keep the old
# one, which still opens what it sealed. Whatever was stored under other
# keys can no longer be read: connect those accounts again.
#CREDENTIAL_KEYS=1:
